7. Link duplicate occurrences to existing bookmarks.
8. Apply explicitly selected categories to new records.
9. Save source occurrence metadata.
10. Update import-job statistics.
11. Show the resulting import summary.

Use database transactions and conflict-aware inserts/upserts.

Do not use a separate in-memory uniqueness map as the only deduplication safeguard.

Never allow two concurrent imports to create two canonical bookmarks with the same identity.

### Large File Handling

The owner has previously provided exports containing approximately 7,485 raw bookmark entries in total.

Build the workflow so it can reasonably handle thousands of entries without freezing the UI.

Use:

- Bounded database insert batches.
- Import checkpoints when necessary.
- Chunk-based writes or resumable operations where appropriate.
- Clear loading/progress states.
- Retry-safe commit behavior.

Do not rely on a long-running, fragile serverless request that assumes unlimited execution time.

Avoid adding Redis, external job queues, or separate worker infrastructure for this basic version unless absolutely required.

Do not introduce background metadata fetching during import.

### Failure Behavior

An interrupted import must not silently lose provenance or create duplicate canonical records on retry.

Record failures honestly.

If some chunks have committed, report the actual partial state and allow a safe resume.

Do not falsely report a failed or partially committed import as fully rolled back.

